<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gilbert Carter</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Gilbert</a> Carter was a British colonial administrator who served as governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> Colony from 1891 to 1897. He forced a treaty on Ijebu after a military expedition in 1892 and toured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> interior in 1893, imposing peace terms that ended the long Yoruba wars between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a> and the Ekiti Parapo, and extending British influence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gilbert-carter.jpg" alt="Gilbert Carter" width="306" height="377" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Bassano · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Gilbert_Thomas_Gilbert-Carter_by_Alexander_Bassano_1893_NPG_x6206_detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gilbert Thomas Carter was born in Topsham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, on 14 January 1848, the only son of Commander Thomas Gilbert Carter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>, and was educated at the Royal Hospital School in Greenwich. He joined the navy in 1864 as an assistant clerk and rose to assistant paymaster in 1869. Serving from 1870 on the colonial steamer Sherbro on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a>, he saw war there and, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elmina">Elmina</a> to the British, was a commissioner valuing the stores and ordnance they left behind. He left the navy in 1875.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After serving as private secretary to Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-berkeley">George Berkeley</a>, Governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leeward-islands">Leeward Islands</a>, he was Collector of Customs and Treasurer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> from 1879 to 1882. He then ran the settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>, becoming its Administrator on 1 December 1888 when the colony was separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-leone">Sierra Leone</a>. After the Anglo-French Convention of 1889 fixed the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, he set up a boundary commission and tried to protect British-aligned towns from the raids of Fode Kaba and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-molo">Musa Molo</a>.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a>, where he became governor on 3 February 1891, he justified his attack on the Ijebu as a war to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and promote civilisation. On his armed tour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorubaland">Yorubaland</a> he was poorly received at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibadan">Ibadan</a> chiefs, fearing their slaves would run to a British Resident, refused a treaty imposing one. In January 1893 the Egba chiefs signed a Treaty of Independence guaranteeing free trade with Lagos and protection for Christian ministers. He was knighted that June for the mission. In 1896 the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a>, Adelekan, gave him Ife works of art, among them the three Ife marbles, which he sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Carter went on to govern the Bahamas and then Barbados from 1904, and administered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> in the governor's absence from 1907 until he retired in 1910. His first wife, Susan Laura Hocker, died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagos">Lagos</a> in 1895; in 1903 he married the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> artist Gertrude Codman Parker. In 1919 he took the surname Gilbert-Carter. He died on 18 January 1927 at Ilaro Court in Barbados, the house his wife had designed. The Carter Bridge, first built in 1901 between Lagos Island and Iddo Island, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gilbert Thomas Carter” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Missionaries and Lagos officials brokered a peace in 1886, but full settlement came only in 1893, when the Governor of the Lagos Colony, <b>Gilbert Carter</b>, imposed terms.</blockquote><a href="/state/ekiti-parapo">Ekiti Parapo</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Topsham, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Barbados. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.69,-3.47&amp;year=1899" aria-label="Topsham on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4471 404 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Gilbert Carter"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4921 674h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4921 674h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.69,-3.47&amp;year=1899">What was here in 1899? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
